\ A definition made inside a quotation dispatched as a runtime-selected value is exempt from the
\ collision guards, the same way a spliced one is. `run` cannot see its argument's body, so the
\ call reaches the quotation's own compiled function rather than splicing it, and that function
\ opens the transient lexical frame itself.
\
\ Neither shadowing definition is ever called. A body that calls its own nested definition is not
\ AOT-compilable, so a call would turn these runtime-legality pins into build failures.

run: ( q -- ) [ call ] ;

\ A quotation the callee picks up at runtime.
[ nip: ( -- y ) [ 1 ] ; ] run

\ A closure over the same shape: `curry` decomposes into a segment over the base's compiled
\ function, so the base's own prologue is what brackets it.
42 [ drop over: ( -- y ) [ 2 ] ; ] curry run

"after" print-line
